==Trivia==
*His full name, Rich Ost, is a pun on ''ostrich''.
*His Japanese name, Dachō Dōkōkai (ダチョウ同好会, "Ostrich Club"), is a reference to the comedy trio [[wikipedia:ja:ダチョウ倶楽部|Dachō Club]] (ダチョウ倶楽部 ''Dachō Kurabu'', "Ostrich Club"), which may be why [[M. Ost]], [[Oldstrich]] and [[Poultrio]] have comedy-related descriptions. The Japanese names of R.Ost and [[Pure Gold R.Ost]] can even be combined to form the comedy trio's name:
**<u>ダチョウ</u>同好会 ''<u>Dachō</u> Dōkōkai'' + 純金<u>倶楽部</u> ''Junkin <u>Kurabu</u>'' = ダチョウ倶楽部 ''Dachō Kurabu''
*He is one of the few [[The Battle Cats|Battle Cats]] characters to change genders depending on the language. He is treated as female in his Spanish description, which says that his first name is Angelita (a female Spanish name), but is male in some other translations.
*Though R.Ost and most of his variants appear to peck twice when they attack, you'll notice they don't have multi-hit, and as such only one peck does damage while the other's their backswing. This allows you to time units easier in stages such as [[Realm of Carnage (Deadly)|Realm of Carnage]] and [[Wrath of Carnage (Deadly)|Wrath of Carnage]].
*All of the R.Osts (except [[Commander Ostromeda]]) relate to comedy in some way.
**R.Ost and Pure Gold R.Ost reference the comedy trio Dachō Club in their Japanese names, as previously mentioned.
**M. Ost is a veteran stand-up comic.
**Oldstrich is a comedian who traded all feelings for the ultimate punchline.
**Poultrio is a comedy trio.

